1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:01,880
An I.V.R.

2
00:00:01,880 --> 00:00:06,043
menu lets callers choose where their call goes by pressing a key.

3
00:00:06,043 --> 00:00:11,203
This video shows the welcome message and the phone menu of an inbound entry point:

4
00:00:11,203 --> 00:00:15,484
how to build a menu with submenus, what happens when a caller makes

5
00:00:15,484 --> 00:00:19,120
a mistake, and how an AI voice agent can answer instead.

6
00:00:23,720 --> 00:00:28,382
Without a menu, every caller reaches the same queue, and agents spend the

7
00:00:28,382 --> 00:00:31,450
first minute sending people to the right team.

8
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:34,898
A phone menu lets the caller choose:

9
00:00:34,898 --> 00:00:37,789
press 1 for sales, press 2 for support.

10
00:00:37,789 --> 00:00:41,900
The call then reaches the right queue or person by itself.

11
00:00:42,650 --> 00:00:46,819
A welcome message greets every caller before the menu, so they know they

12
00:00:46,819 --> 00:00:48,530
reached the right company.

13
00:00:52,730 --> 00:00:55,627
A menu is a prompt and a list of keys.

14
00:00:55,627 --> 00:01:01,420
The prompt is spoken to the caller, or a recording from Voice media plays instead.

15
00:01:02,170 --> 00:01:04,240
Each key has an action:

16
00:01:04,240 --> 00:01:08,665
send to a queue, send to an agent, open a submenu, go to

17
00:01:08,665 --> 00:01:15,730
another menu, send to voicemail, transfer to an approved external number, or repeat the menu.

18
00:01:16,480 --> 00:01:20,577
Tries limit how many wrong or missing keys a caller gets, and a

19
00:01:20,577 --> 00:01:24,436
fallback says where the caller goes when the tries run out.

20
00:01:24,436 --> 00:01:26,633
Callers answer with the keypad.

21
00:01:26,633 --> 00:01:28,770
Speech input is not supported.

22
00:01:33,070 --> 00:01:36,896
Open the entry point, and find the Welcome and IVR menu card.

23
00:01:36,896 --> 00:01:41,235
The Welcome message is spoken once to every caller who rings while the

24
00:01:41,235 --> 00:01:42,720
entry point is open.

25
00:01:43,933 --> 00:01:46,096
It plays before the first menu.

26
00:01:46,096 --> 00:01:50,480
With no menu, it plays before the caller is put through to the queue.

27
00:01:50,480 --> 00:01:55,623
The queue has a welcome message of its own, spoken when the caller starts waiting.

28
00:01:59,923 --> 00:02:02,285
Click Build an IVR menu.

29
00:02:02,285 --> 00:02:07,243
A Main menu appears, marked First menu, with one key row.

30
00:02:07,806 --> 00:02:12,200
In What callers hear, type the prompt, and mention every key:

31
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:16,201
press 1 for sales, 2 for support, 3 to talk to Jamie, 4

32
00:02:16,201 --> 00:02:19,086
for billing, and 9 to leave a message.

33
00:02:19,811 --> 00:02:24,154
For each choice, set the Key, the Action and the Target.

34
00:02:24,154 --> 00:02:28,008
Key 1 uses Send to a queue, with the Sales queue.

35
00:02:28,008 --> 00:02:32,001
Click Add key, and key 2 goes to the Support queue.

36
00:02:37,280 --> 00:02:40,836
Key 3 uses Send to an agent, and key 9 uses Send to

37
00:02:40,836 --> 00:02:45,390
voicemail, which follows the voicemail settings of the entry point.

38
00:02:52,329 --> 00:02:55,802
Instead of speaking the text, pick a Recorded prompt.

39
00:02:55,802 --> 00:02:58,569
It lists the recordings from Voice media.

40
00:03:02,869 --> 00:03:06,326
To group choices, use the action Open a submenu.

41
00:03:06,326 --> 00:03:10,423
Key 4, for billing, now opens a menu of its own, which you

42
00:03:10,423 --> 00:03:12,151
fill in the same way:

43
00:03:12,151 --> 00:03:14,199
a prompt and its own keys.

44
00:03:14,658 --> 00:03:19,087
In the submenu, key 1 sends callers to the Billing queue.

45
00:03:19,087 --> 00:03:23,588
Key 0 uses Go to another menu, to return to the main menu.

46
00:03:27,134 --> 00:03:29,707
Repeat this menu plays the prompt again.

47
00:03:29,707 --> 00:03:34,181
Transfer to an approved external number connects the caller to a number on

48
00:03:34,181 --> 00:03:37,984
the approved destinations list in the Contact Center settings.

49
00:03:42,284 --> 00:03:47,569
At the top, Tries is how many wrong or missing keys a caller is allowed.

50
00:03:47,569 --> 00:03:51,904
A wrong key, no key, or Repeat this menu each use one try.

51
00:03:52,669 --> 00:03:57,478
When the tries run out, the default is Route to the entry point target.

52
00:03:57,478 --> 00:04:02,849
You can send the caller to a queue, an agent, voicemail or another menu instead.

53
00:04:25,370 --> 00:04:29,880
The builder counts the problems it finds, and the entry point does not

54
00:04:29,880 --> 00:04:31,779
save until they are fixed.

55
00:04:31,779 --> 00:04:35,695
A menu needs a prompt and at least one key, and queue, agent

56
00:04:35,695 --> 00:04:37,950
and transfer keys need a target.

57
00:04:38,404 --> 00:04:39,452
Click Save.

58
00:04:39,452 --> 00:04:44,504
Callers hear the menu straight away, and only while the entry point is open.

59
00:04:48,704 --> 00:04:52,706
Instead of a menu, a call entry point can hand its calls to

60
00:04:52,706 --> 00:04:54,184
an AI voice agent:

61
00:04:54,184 --> 00:04:58,494
an AI profile that greets the caller and holds the conversation.

62
00:04:59,244 --> 00:05:03,590
When the Telnyx AI Voice Agent feature is on, Route to on the

63
00:05:03,590 --> 00:05:08,521
Routing card offers AI voice agent, with the AI agent profile to pick.

64
00:05:08,521 --> 00:05:11,894
The welcome message and the menu are not used.

65
00:05:12,644 --> 00:05:16,444
The AI can pass a caller to a person with the hand-off set

66
00:05:16,444 --> 00:05:17,988
on the subject flow.

67
00:05:17,988 --> 00:05:22,619
While the entry point is closed, callers go to voicemail, or are refused

68
00:05:22,619 --> 00:05:24,934
when the closed action is Reject.

69
00:05:29,134 --> 00:05:34,862
A caller rings an open entry point, hears the welcome message once, then the main menu.

70
00:05:34,862 --> 00:05:37,634
A key moves them to the choice you set.

71
00:05:38,384 --> 00:05:42,161
A wrong key plays the menu again until the tries run out.

72
00:05:42,161 --> 00:05:46,597
A choice that cannot be reached, such as a disabled queue, sends the

73
00:05:46,597 --> 00:05:50,314
caller to the entry point target, and then to voicemail.

74
00:05:54,514 --> 00:05:57,084
A small business with one number:

75
00:05:57,084 --> 00:05:59,654
press 1 for sales, 2 for support.

76
00:06:00,404 --> 00:06:05,544
A clinic with a billing submenu, so callers pick invoices or payments.

77
00:06:06,294 --> 00:06:10,714
A team that is often busy, with a key that leaves a voicemail.

78
00:06:11,464 --> 00:06:15,902
A company that wants a greeting recorded by a person, using a Recorded

79
00:06:15,902 --> 00:06:17,654
prompt from Voice media.

80
00:06:19,654 --> 00:06:24,009
Write the prompt, give each key an action, and callers reach the right

81
00:06:24,009 --> 00:06:26,014
queue or person without help.

82
00:06:26,014 --> 00:06:27,504
Thanks for watching.

